From c26a57d999079cf35c7ded19e9e4159f8088d361 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 17:27:24 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Emit the bitwise operators natively, item 3-1's premise
 measurement
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

3-8 promoted 3-1 to the front of phase 3 on a census: 42.01% of the corpus's
allocation is number boxing (corrected here from 41.89% by counting the
JSNumber constructor as well as the factory — a builtin writing `new
JSNumber(x)` directly is 0.3% of all boxes). That promotion sat against 3-1's
own finding that a typed backing store is a wash. Both are right, and
reconciling them is what the item needed first.

The element chain decomposes exactly, every term a multiple of 32 B, against
a raw-double control at 0.00: `s = s + a[0]` is 31.98, `a[0] * 1.5` is 64.00,
`s = s + a[0] * 1.5` is 95.99 (= 64 + 32, to the hundredth), and a
read-modify-write chain is 159.67. The element STORE is in none of it. The
boxes are minted by the operators, and the read is free today only because
what it hands back is already a box — so a typed store alone stays a wash and
the item that pays is storage PLUS an unboxed element read.

Two things fell out. A numeric literal is re-boxed on every evaluation —
VisitLiteral has statics for NaN, 0, 1 and 2 only — so `a[0] * 1.5` costs two
boxes where `a[0] * 2` costs one. Counted through a separate factory entry
that is 1 671 331 of 133 936 952 requests, 1.2%: real, and recorded rather
than built.

And the bitwise and shift operators had no native form although
NumericLocalAnalysis has always typed them: `s = i + 1023` costs 0.00 B/iter
and `s = i & 1023` cost 31.84, with both operands raw doubles and the result
stored straight back into one. That half is built. JSNumericOperators routes
all six through JSValue.ToUint32 — the same helper IntValue uses — because a
bitwise operand is ToInt32/ToUint32 of the double and that reduction is not a
CLR cast, which is undefined on overflow rather than wrapping.

On its shape it removes the box completely (31.84 -> 0.00, and the
read-modify-write chain 159.67 -> 96.00). On the corpus it removes nothing:
six suites identical to the digit, and Crypto — a BigInteger implementation
built on &, | and >> — differing by less than its own run-to-run variation,
which was measured by running one arm twice (42 418 727 against 42 421 217;
it generates RSA keys). The native form needs both operands native and
Crypto's digits live in this.array[i].

That is item 3-5's finding from a second direction. Six items have now built
machinery array-resident data cannot reach, every one correct and every one
invisible, and every one waiting on 3-1.

NativeBitwiseOperatorTests is 15 cases — ToInt32 wrapping, NaN and both
infinities, shift-count masking, >>>'s unsigned result, the coercions a
non-numeric operand must still get, and a getter that must run exactly once —
each asserted on both settings of BROILER_JS_NATIVE_BITWISE. 7 925 tests pass.
